  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 427 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe 00Z upper air analysis shows the trough behind this week's\nheavy rain exiting into the Ohio Valley, leaving northwest flow\naloft between it and a ridge building over the southwestern CONUS.\nWater vapor imagery shows a dry column overhead with surface high\npressure ridge centered through the CWA. Friday verified at the\nwarm end of the forecast, with highs of 70 to 72 and afternoon\ndewpoints mixing into the upper 30s and low 40s, supporting another \nslight warm lean today. Expect a crisp early fall morning in the 40s,\nabundant sunshine, and highs in the low 70s. Confidence in a dry, \nquiet day is high.\n\nPatchy fog early this morning has stayed shallow and confined to\nlow-lying areas, with the drier air mass limiting development\nbeyond the typically cooler spots. Area VWPs also show slightly\nstronger winds just off the surface, with that gradual mixing\nhelping to keep fog development in check. Another round of patchy\nvalley fog cannot be ruled out around sunrise Sunday, though a\nweak frontal boundary sagging into the area tonight and a similar\nlow-level wind profile both work against anything widespread.\nImpacts stay low, with any fog gone shortly after sunrise.\n\nSoutherly return flow develops behind the departing high and holds\nthrough much of the week. Warm air advection and a slow return of\nmodified Gulf moisture gradually raise dewpoints, so the air mass\nturns more humid even as rain chances stay near zero beneath the\nbroad ridging. Height plumes hold steady through midweek and every\nWPC cluster keeps positive anomalies anchored over the Plains,\nsupporting highs climbing from the low 70s into the low 80s for\nsome by midweek. Strong ensemble agreement keeps confidence in the\ndry, warming trend high.\n\nThe first cracks appear late in the week as height plumes begin a\nslow decline and member spread widens beyond Thursday. Clusters\ndiffer on the next Pacific trough's depth and timing but keep the\narea dry into next weekend, pointing to a flattening ridge rather\nthan a regime change. The seasonal transition appears to be\nstarting with the more shallow moisture return, but the true air\nmass change still waits on an upstream trough bringing a more\nmeaningful push of cooler Canadian air behind it.",
